WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.080 --> 00:00:07.080
A seductive stranger with a stack of secrets,
a mysterious mansion, and an aspiring American author

00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:11.620
all feature in director Guillermo del Toro's 
gothic romance Crimson Peak.

00:00:11.620 --> 00:00:15.920
I'm Jan and this is 10 things you probably
didn't know about Crimson Peak.

00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:22.300
Director Guillermo del Toro wrote 10-page
character biographies for Jessica Chastain,

00:00:22.300 --> 00:00:24.500
Mia Wasikowska, and Tom Hiddleston.

00:00:24.500 --> 00:00:28.560
The in-depth biographies included details
such as the character's upbringing,

00:00:28.560 --> 00:00:32.320
relationships with their parents, 
favorite smells, and secrets,

00:00:32.320 --> 00:00:35.940
all of which del Toro told each actor
to keep to him or herself.

00:00:35.940 --> 00:00:39.460
The information not only helped 
the stars prepare for their roles,

00:00:39.460 --> 00:00:42.180
but also ensured they were 
on the same page as del Toro

00:00:42.180 --> 00:00:46.640
when it came to understanding 
their character's actions, influences, and motivations.

00:00:48.220 --> 00:00:52.020
When Tom Hiddleston replaced Benedict Cumberbatch 
in the role of Thomas Sharpe,

00:00:52.020 --> 00:00:54.200
Guillermo del Toro rewrote the script.

00:00:54.200 --> 00:00:58.180
According to del Toro, before 
Hiddleston joined the Crimson Peak cast,

00:00:58.180 --> 00:01:01.360
Thomas was originally
going to be a much colder character.

00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:03.880
On top of Del Toro's character biography,

00:01:03.880 --> 00:01:09.340
Hiddleston found inspiration in a series of mood boards created by costume designer Kate Hawley,

00:01:09.340 --> 00:01:11.840
which he placed around 
his trailer during filming.

00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:16.900
The boards included pictures of English poet 
Lord Byron, Victorian engineers and miners,

00:01:16.900 --> 00:01:22.580
and a painting called Wanderer Above The Sea Of Fog by German Romantic artist Caspar David Friedrich.

00:01:24.280 --> 00:01:27.960
When del Toro first sent Jessica Chastain
the script for Crimson Peak,

00:01:27.960 --> 00:01:32.460
he assumed she'd want to play the main character
Edith, an aspiring American author.

00:01:32.460 --> 00:01:37.240
But Chastain was more interested in the role
of the mysterious antagonist, Lucille,

00:01:37.240 --> 00:01:40.080
who's the sister of 
Tom Hiddleston's character, Thomas.

00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:42.340
Crimson Peak was 
a tough shoot for Chastain,

00:01:42.340 --> 00:01:46.760
as not only did she have to get into the dark
headspace necessary to play Lucille,

00:01:46.760 --> 00:01:50.320
but she was also filming A Most Violent Year 
at the same time.

00:01:50.320 --> 00:01:54.340
So, when it was time to shoot 
an especially adrenaline-charged scene,

00:01:54.340 --> 00:01:59.020
Del Toro suggested Chastain drink several 
large espressos to get in the right mood!

00:01:59.020 --> 00:02:02.980
Oh, and by the way, Chastain also learnt 
to play the piano for the film!

00:02:04.460 --> 00:02:08.660
Not long after Charlie Hunnam finished filming
Pacific Rim with Guillermo del Toro,

00:02:08.660 --> 00:02:12.380
the director sent Hunnam an email 
asking him to star in Crimson Peak.

00:02:12.380 --> 00:02:15.980
From then on in, Hunnam and del Toro 
worked together to create a biography

00:02:15.980 --> 00:02:19.700
for Hunnam's character, a young American 
doctor called Alan McMichael.

00:02:19.700 --> 00:02:23.220
The one thing Del Toro wasn't keen on 
that Hunnam suggested

00:02:23.220 --> 00:02:25.740
was that Dr McMichael 
should be a pipe-smoker.

00:02:25.740 --> 00:02:29.480
According to Hunnam, the reason was that 
del Toro didn't want to add pipe-smoking

00:02:29.480 --> 00:02:32.720
to the list of things he had to fight 
the studio to get on screen.

00:02:34.100 --> 00:02:38.060
The movie's ghosts were played by actors 
Doug Jones and Javier Botet,

00:02:38.060 --> 00:02:41.360
whose physical presence on set 
really helped the movie's leads

00:02:41.360 --> 00:02:43.660
as it gave them something 
real to react to.

00:02:43.660 --> 00:02:47.420
The transparent look of the ghosts 
was later added with CGI.

00:02:47.420 --> 00:02:52.600
Jones and Botet have both brought a number
of creatures to life in Del Toro's previous productions,

00:02:52.600 --> 00:02:55.160
including Pan's Labyrinth, 
Hellboy, and Mama.

00:02:56.540 --> 00:03:00.860
To bring to life the gothic mansion 
Allerdale Hall, aka Crimson Peak,

00:03:00.860 --> 00:03:04.260
a huge set was built 
at Pinewood Toronto Studios.

00:03:04.260 --> 00:03:08.140
It took 6 months to design and 
build the three-and-a-half-storey set,

00:03:08.140 --> 00:03:12.180
which not only had a working elevator,
but also functioning fireplaces.

00:03:12.180 --> 00:03:14.760
The mansion's rooms were 
linked by hallways,

00:03:14.760 --> 00:03:17.900
which meant the camera could move 
seamlessly around the house

00:03:17.900 --> 00:03:22.800
and del Toro could avoid having to 
digitally stitch scenes together in post-production.

00:03:22.800 --> 00:03:26.960
The only room that wasn't connected 
to the main mansion set was the bedroom.

00:03:28.520 --> 00:03:31.340
One of the only parts of the house 
that needed green screen

00:03:31.340 --> 00:03:34.080
was the hole in the ceiling
in order to show the sky.

00:03:34.080 --> 00:03:37.080
Although the mansion's floor 
looks like it's made of wood,

00:03:37.080 --> 00:03:39.680
it was actually made of 
especially strong concrete,

00:03:39.680 --> 00:03:43.220
so it could withstand the volume of crew 
moving about throughout the shoot.

00:03:43.220 --> 00:03:46.220
The Crimson Peak mansion 
is del Toro's favourite set,

00:03:46.220 --> 00:03:49.880
so he was particularly sad 
when it had to be taken apart after filming.

00:03:51.480 --> 00:03:55.380
Some of the pieces of furniture made 
for the movie were built in two different sizes,

00:03:55.380 --> 00:03:57.300
one bigger and one smaller.

00:03:57.300 --> 00:04:02.320
So, when Del Toro wanted to emphasise the fact 
that a character was weak in a particular scene,

00:04:02.320 --> 00:04:05.120
they'd use the larger sized furniture on set.

00:04:05.120 --> 00:04:08.100
However, if he wanted to make 
the character appear stronger,

00:04:08.100 --> 00:04:10.920
he'd use the smaller version
of the piece of furniture.

00:04:12.340 --> 00:04:16.060
The word "Fear" and the motif 
of butterflies versus moths

00:04:16.060 --> 00:04:20.040
are hidden throughout the design of the house, 
including in the custom-made wallpaper.

00:04:20.040 --> 00:04:24.060
Del Toro has been fascinated by butterflies
and moths since he was a child

00:04:24.060 --> 00:04:29.200
and used them to allude to the differences between 
the film's two main female characters, Lucille and Edith.

00:04:30.680 --> 00:04:34.960
When Del Toro and his co-writer Matthew Robbins
were penning the Crimson Peak script,

00:04:34.960 --> 00:04:39.220
they were inspired by an array of novels 
including Emily Brontë's Wuthering Heights,

00:04:39.220 --> 00:04:43.300
Charles Dickens' Great Expectations, 
Daphne du Maurier's Rebecca,

00:04:43.300 --> 00:04:45.139
Charlotte Brontë's Jane Eyre,

00:04:45.139 --> 00:04:50.079
and one of Del Toro's favourite gothic novels,
Joseph Sheridan Le Fanu's Uncle Silas.

00:04:50.080 --> 00:04:55.020
In fact, the character Mia Wasikowska 
plays in Crimson Peak is named Edith

00:04:55.020 --> 00:04:59.560
after American author Edith Wharton, 
whose novels include The Age Of Innocence.

00:04:59.560 --> 00:05:02.040
And given Del Toro's love of Frankenstein

00:05:02.040 --> 00:05:05.040
and the fact that author Mary Shelley 
is mentioned in the film,

00:05:05.040 --> 00:05:10.140
it's quite possible that Edith's surname Cushing 
is a reference to actor Peter Cushing,

00:05:10.140 --> 00:05:13.160
who played Frankenstein 
in many Hammer Horror movies.

00:05:13.160 --> 00:05:17.260
Now, let me know in the comments below, 
which are your favourite Guillermo del Toro movies?

00:05:17.260 --> 00:05:20.520
And what are the spookiest stories 
you've seen on screen?

